User logs in (credentials → /api/auth/login)
1.Client sends username/password.
2.Server authenticates credentials (DB/UserDetailsService).
3.If OK → server generates JWT (signed, contains username, expiration, roles).
4.Server returns JWT to client (usually in JSON body).
{ "token": "eyJhbGciOi..." }
5.Client stores token (in-memory, secure cookie with HttpOnly/secure, or localStorage — each has tradeoffs).
6.Client sends token on subsequent requests in Authorization header:
Authorization: Bearer <jwt>
7.Server receives request, extracts token from header (or cookie), validates it:
8.Verify signature (using secret/public key)
Check exp (not expired)
Optionally check iat, nbf, issuer (iss), audience (aud)
Optionally check token is not revoked / blacklisted
9.If valid → server sets SecurityContext / user principal and processes request as authenticated user.
10.If invalid/expired → return 401 Unauthorized. If using refresh tokens, client can call refresh endpoint to obtain a new access token.

Token validation details (server-side)
Extract token from Authorization header or cookie.
Parse header+payload, verify signature using secret (HMAC) or public key (RSA/ECDSA).
Confirm exp > now (not expired).
Optionally check: nbf (not before), iss (issuer), aud (audience), scope/roles.
Optionally check revocation (blacklist/store of invalidated tokens) or token version in DB.

If everything OK → user is authenticated.
